

P1/P2 — onlyDuringBusinessHours is not enforced

Rules support:

onlyDuringBusinessHours: z.boolean().default(true)

But checkJob() and processDueJobs() do not check business hours.

So a rule configured as business-hours-only can still send at midnight if the job is due.

Required fix

When job is due:

If onlyDuringBusinessHours = true
and current business local time is outside open hours
→ reschedule to next opening time

Expected behavior:

scheduledFor = 10:00 PM
business hours = 8:00 AM–5:00 PM
→ do not send at 10:00 PM
→ reschedule to next business day 8:00 AM

Use the business timezone, not server timezone.

P2 — Follow-up rule creation does not enforce useAiRewrite by tier

Policy says:

aiRewriteAllowed: subscription.plan.code !== PlanCode.BASIC

But createRule() and updateRule() do not block:

useAiRewrite: true

for Basic.

Even if AI rewrite is not implemented yet, the setting should not be accepted on Basic if product says it is Plus/Premium.

Required fix

In create/update:

if (input.useAiRewrite && !policy.aiRewriteAllowed) {
  throw new AppError(
    403,
    "AI rewrite is not available on your current plan.",
    "PLAN_UPGRADE_REQUIRED"
  );
}
P2 — maxSendsPerLead and maxSendsPerConversation are client-controlled beyond policy

The schema allows:

maxSendsPerLead: z.number().int().min(1).max(50).default(1),
maxSendsPerConversation: z.number().int().min(1).max(20).default(1),

But the policy says:

Basic:
maxSendsPerConversation = 1
maxSendsPerLead = 2

Plus:
maxSendsPerConversation = 3
maxSendsPerLead = 8

Premium:
maxSendsPerConversation = 5
maxSendsPerLead = 20

createRule() does not clamp input to policy limits.

So a Basic user can submit:

maxSendsPerLead = 50
maxSendsPerConversation = 20
Required fix

After loading policy:

if (input.maxSendsPerLead > policy.maxSendsPerLead) {
  throw new AppError(403, "Max sends per lead exceeds your plan limit.", "PLAN_LIMIT_REACHED");
}

if (input.maxSendsPerConversation > policy.maxSendsPerConversation) {
  throw new AppError(403, "Max sends per conversation exceeds your plan limit.", "PLAN_LIMIT_REACHED");
}

Or clamp silently, but I prefer explicit rejection.

P2 — cooldownMinutes exists but is not enforced

The schema supports:

cooldownMinutes

But I do not see any enforcement in scheduling or processing.

That means a rule can have cooldown configured, but it has no effect.

Required fix

Before scheduling or sending, check the last send log for:

same business
same rule
same lead/conversation
deliveryStatus SENT
createdAt within cooldown window

If cooldown is active:

skip or reschedule


P2 — Duplicate job prevention is not strong enough

Current duplicate check:

where: {
  businessId,
  ruleId,
  conversationId: input.conversationId ?? null,
  contextType: input.contextType,
  relatedMessageId: input.relatedMessageId ?? null,
  status: SCHEDULED,
}

Issues:

It does not include leadId.
It does not include appointmentId.
It does not include quoteId.
If conversationId is null, duplicate logic can behave badly depending on Prisma/null matching.
There is no DB uniqueness constraint.

Possible duplicate:

Same rule + same lead + no conversation
→ two scheduled jobs can exist
Required fix

Use a deterministic dedupeKey.

Example:

dedupeKey = hash(
  businessId +
  ruleId +
  contextType +
  leadId +
  conversationId +
  appointmentId +
  quoteId +
  relatedMessageId
)

Then enforce:

@@unique([businessId, dedupeKey, status])

If partial unique indexes are available, enforce only for SCHEDULED.

P2 — Cancelling job is stale-write prone

cancelJob() does:

const job = await prisma.followUpJob.findFirst(...)

if (job.status !== SCHEDULED) return job;

const updated = await prisma.followUpJob.update({
  where: { id: job.id },
  data: { status: CANCELLED }
});

Race:

User cancels job
Processor sends job at same time
Cancel writes after send or send writes after cancel
Final status may be wrong
Required fix

Use updateMany() with status condition:

const changed = await prisma.followUpJob.updateMany({
  where: {
    id: job.id,
    businessId: actor.businessId,
    status: FollowUpJobStatus.SCHEDULED,
  },
  data: {
    status: FollowUpJobStatus.CANCELLED,
    cancelReason: reason,
  },
});

if (changed.count !== 1) {
  throw new AppError(409, "Follow-up job changed. Refresh and try again.", "FOLLOW_UP_JOB_STATE_CHANGED");
}
P2 — Disabling automation cancels jobs without audit/detail per job

When disabling automation:

await prisma.followUpJob.updateMany({
  where: { businessId: actor.businessId, status: SCHEDULED },
  data: { status: CANCELLED, cancelReason: "FOLLOW_UP_AUTOMATION_DISABLED" },
});

This is okay, but:

No count returned to frontend
No per-job realtime event
No clear job-level audit trail

At minimum, return:

cancelledJobCount

and publish a broad event:

business.follow_up.jobs.cancelled_bulk

Not a blocker, but useful.

P2 — Inbound reply evaluation only cancels, despite action type supporting reschedule/replace

The type supports:

action: "CANCEL_FOLLOW_UP" | "KEEP_FOLLOW_UP" | "RESCHEDULE_FOLLOW_UP" | "REPLACE_WITH_NEW_FOLLOW_UP"

But evaluateInboundReply() only handles:

if (result.action === "CANCEL_FOLLOW_UP") {
  updateMany(... CANCELLED ...)
}

No support for:

RESCHEDULE_FOLLOW_UP
REPLACE_WITH_NEW_FOLLOW_UP

That is acceptable for base foundation, but then either:

remove unsupported actions from the type

or implement them later before exposing.


P2/P3 — Pending follow-up context is included in AI prompt, but AI does not control jobs

The AI prompt says:

Pending follow-up contexts are present. If the latest customer message does not resolve them, keep the unresolved request naturally in the reply.

That is good for conversation behavior.

But actual cancellation is deterministic/regex-based in followUpContextEvaluationService.

So if AI understands the reply but regex does not, job may remain scheduled.

Example:

Pending email request:
Customer says: “Send it to my work mail, same one I gave yesterday.”

AI may understand, but regex may not.

This is okay for V1, but for Plus/Premium, consider AI-assisted follow-up context resolution.

P3 — Default rules are seeded with Promise.all

In seedDefaultRulesForBusiness():

await Promise.all(defaults.map((rule) => db.followUpAutomationRule.create(...)))

If called twice concurrently, duplicate default rules can be created unless DB uniqueness prevents it.

Since ensureDefaultRulesForBusiness() checks count first, this is race-prone.

Fix

Use upsert or a unique key:

businessId + type

Then seed defaults idempotently.

P3 — Staff visibility for unassigned conversation follow-ups needs UX confirmation

Staff can see jobs/logs where the linked conversation is:

assigned to them
or unassigned

This mirrors conversation access.

That is safe if staff are allowed to see unassigned queue work. Frontend should show unassigned follow-ups as read-only or claim-needed if they cannot act.

What is good for a base foundation

This is a good base layer:

Rule model
Job model
Send log model
Plan policy skeleton
Default seeded rules
Inbound reply cancellation
AI context awareness
Realtime events
Business setting toggle
Basic/Plus/Premium rule-type separation

The main missing piece is that it is not yet a real sending engine. It is currently closer to:

follow-up rules + scheduled job queue + send logs foundation

not:

full follow-up automation delivery system
